.TH DARPA-SPILL-SHIFT 1 "October 2026" "darpa_spillserver 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
darpa-spill-shift \- blur event timestamps reversibly with a secret token (dts1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B darpa-spill-shift token
.B \-\-dist flat \-\-width
.I DURATION
|
.B \-\-dist gauss \-\-sigma
.I DURATION
.RB [ \-\-cut
.IR DURATION | N sigma]
.RB [ \-\-seed
.IR TEXT ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.PP
.B darpa-spill-shift apply
.RB [ \-\-token\-file
.IR FILE ]
.IR FILE ...
.PP
.B darpa-spill-shift invert
.RB [ \-\-token\-file
.IR FILE ]
.IR FILE ...
.PP
.B darpa-spill-shift info
.RI [ TOKEN | FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B darpa-spill-shift
moves the timestamp of every event in a
.BR darpa-spill-dump (1)
file by a small pseudorandom offset, drawn from a flat or a truncated Gaussian
distribution and never larger than \(+-500 ms, and can undo the shift
exactly.  The offsets are derived from a secret
.IR token :
whoever holds the token can invert the file; whoever does not sees only the
blurred times.
.PP
The transform is called
.BR dts1 .
Its specification, proofs of invertibility, statistical tests and a
discussion of what it hides are in
.I docs/TimestampShift.nb
(Mathematica), with a summary in
.IR docs/TIMESTAMP_SHIFT.md .
.SS How it works
Each hardware event, identified by
.RB ( source ", " event_number ),
gets its own offset
.IR o ( n )
from HMAC-SHA256 of the token's secret.  Those two columns are not changed,
so the offset can be recomputed from a shifted row and subtracted.
.PP
.RS
.nf
nova_time'  = nova_time + o(n)
pps_offset' = (pps_offset + o(n)) mod 1 s
delta'      = delta + o(n) \- o(n\-1)
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B pps_offset
is the true sub-second phase and
.B delta
the exact gap to the previous hardware event; either would reveal the true
times if left alone.  Every other time column
.RB ( utc ", " gps ", " unix_sec ,
\&...) is recomputed from the shifted
.BR nova_time .
Other columns are unchanged.
.SS What it does not hide
.B event_number
must stay in the file and numbers events in true order, so order is not
hidden.  Events whose times are predictable (the 1 Hz pulser fires on the
second) can be snapped back to their true times by anyone who knows the
pattern.  See section 11 of the notebook.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B token
Create a token and print it, or write it to
.B \-o
.I FILE
with mode 0600.  The token is the only way to invert data shifted with it:
keep it.
.TP
.B apply
Shift each input file.  Output is
.IB NAME .shifted. EXT
beside the input, or
.B \-o
.I FILE
for a single input.  With
.B \-\-dist
and
.B \-\-token\-out
.IR FILE ,
a new token is created, saved, and used in one step.
.TP
.B invert
Restore each input file.  Output is
.IB NAME .restored. EXT
(a
.B .shifted
in the name is replaced), or
.BR \-o .
The result is byte-identical to the original dump.
.TP
.B info
Describe a token, a token file, or a data file.  With a data file and a
token, report whether the token is the one the file was shifted with (exit
status 1 if not).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS New tokens (token, apply)
.TP
.BR \-\-dist " flat|gauss"
The offset distribution.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " DURATION"
Flat: offsets are uniform on the whole ticks in \(+-DURATION.
.TP
.BI \-\-sigma " DURATION"
Gaussian: the standard deviation before truncation.
.TP
.BI \-\-cut " DURATION\fR|\fPN" sigma
Gaussian: truncate at \(+-DURATION, or at N standard deviations.  Default
.BR 3sigma ,
lowered to 500 ms if 3\(*s would exceed it.  Must lie between \(*s/2 and
500 ms.  The standard deviation after truncation at 3\(*s is 0.987\(*s.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " TEXT\fR, \fP" \-\-seed\-file " FILE"
Derive the secret from TEXT with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (600,000 iterations)
instead of at random.  The same seed and parameters always give the same
token, which is convenient, but anyone who can guess the seed can invert the
data.  Use a generated token when the blur protects something.
.PP
Durations take a unit:
.BR s ", " ms ", " us ", " ns ,
or
.B ticks
(64 MHz, 15.625 ns).  They are rounded to whole ticks.
.SS Existing tokens (apply, invert, info)
.TP
.BI \-\-token " TOKEN"
The token itself.  It is then visible in
.BR ps (1)
and shell history; prefer a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-token\-file " FILE"
Read the token from the first line of FILE.
.SS Files (apply, invert)
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE"
Output for a single input;
.B \-
for standard output.  The input is never overwritten.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Skip the safety checks: shifting an already-shifted file, inverting a file
with no marker, or inverting with a token whose fingerprint differs from the
file's.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing but errors.
.SH FILES
Inputs are CSV or JSON written by
.BR darpa-spill-dump (1)
or the server's
.IR /api/events ,
detected by extension or content; standard input is read as CSV.  They must
include the
.BR nova_time ,
.B source
and
.B event_number
columns.  Files split by
.B \-\-split
can be given all at once.
.PP
A shifted CSV gains a comment line
.PP
.RS
.nf
# darpa-spill-shift: dts1 shifted, params=..., fingerprint=...
.fi
.RE
.PP
and a shifted JSON a
.B meta.shift
object with the same information.  Neither contains the secret; the
fingerprint is a public 64-bit identifier of the token.  Inversion removes
the marker.
.PP
Outputs are written as
.IB FILE .part
and renamed when complete.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_SHIFT_TOKEN
The token, as
.BR \-\-token .
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_SHIFT_TOKEN_FILE
A token file, as
.BR \-\-token\-file .
.PP
Precedence: \-\-token, \-\-token\-file, DARPA_SPILL_SHIFT_TOKEN,
DARPA_SPILL_SHIFT_TOKEN_FILE.  The token is deliberately not read from the
YAML configuration.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be processed: already shifted, wrong token, no marker,
missing columns, unreadable.  No partial output is left.  For
.BR info ,
the token does not match the file.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, or a bad or missing token.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a token for a Gaussian blur of \(*s = 20 ms, cut at 60 ms:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-shift token --dist gauss --sigma 20ms -o ~/nd-blur.tok
.fi
.RE
.PP
Shift a day's dump, then restore it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-shift apply --token-file ~/nd-blur.tok day.csv day.json
darpa-spill-shift invert --token-file ~/nd-blur.tok day.shifted.csv
cmp day.csv day.restored.csv        # identical
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a flat \(+-50 ms token and shift a week of hourly files in one step:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-shift apply --dist flat --width 50ms --token-out week.tok \e
    week/nd_*.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check which token a file needs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa-spill-shift info day.shifted.csv --token-file ~/nd-blur.tok
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR darpa-spill-dump (1),
.BR darpa-spill-query (1)
.PP
.I docs/TimestampShift.nb
and
.I docs/TIMESTAMP_SHIFT.md
in the source tree.
